      <h2>The Intermediate Dead Hang Level</h2>

      <p>On this site, intermediate dead hang training covers holds of 30 to 90 seconds. These bands are the site's own training ranges. They are not published norms. Beginners build a base through simple overhand holds. Intermediate training shifts focus to grip endurance and movement variety.</p>

      <p>Reaching 60 to 90 seconds takes practice with multiple grip types, fatigue management across longer sets and shoulder endurance for extended hanging. The <a href="/8-week-program/">8-week plan framework</a> is a way to compare like-for-like sessions; the schedule below is a worked example.</p>

      <p>Time spent in this range varies a lot. Add time gradually because raising volume too quickly can irritate elbow tendons. The doses on this page are common training practice, not a tested prescription.</p>

      <h2>Grip Endurance Training</h2>

      <p>In intermediate training, endurance replaces maximum effort as the main stimulus. Longer holds at sub-maximal intensity build the ability to hold for longer.</p>

      <h3>Volume Accumulation</h3>

      <p>Total hang time per session can serve as the target. Four sets of 30 seconds gives 120 seconds. Two sets of 45 seconds gives 90. Accumulate 2 to 3 minutes of total hang time per session across 4 sets.</p>

      <h3>Shorter Rest Periods</h3>

      <p>Many people shorten rest from 90 seconds to 60 seconds between sets once sessions feel easy. Shorter recovery asks your grip to perform under more fatigue.</p>

      <h3>Sample Endurance Session</h3>

      <p class="table-cue">Scroll the table horizontally on smaller screens.</p><div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Scrollable comparison table" tabindex="0"><table class="data-table">
        <thead>
