          <tr>
            <td><strong>Set 1</strong></td>
            <td>35-40 seconds</td>
            <td>60 seconds</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Set 2</strong></td>
            <td>30-35 seconds</td>
            <td>60 seconds</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Set 3</strong></td>
            <td>25-30 seconds</td>
            <td>60 seconds</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Set 4</strong></td>
            <td>20-25 seconds (stop a second or two before your grip gives out)</td>
            <td>Done</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>

      <p>Total hang time in this example is 110 to 130 seconds per session. More total hang time across sessions is the usual way to build toward longer holds.</p>

      <h2>Introducing Grip Variations</h2>

      <p>Intermediate training rotates between three grip types to add variety. Each one changes wrist and shoulder position. Your finger flexors do the holding in all three.</p>

      <h3>Underhand Grip (Supinated)</h3>

      <p>Palms face toward you like a chin-up position. The biceps carry more of the load than in an overhand grip. An underhand grip puts the biceps tendon under stretch. Stop if the front of the elbow hurts. Use it on days when your overhand grip feels fatigued.</p>

      <h3>Neutral Grip</h3>

      <p>Palms face each other on parallel handles. Many people find this position comfortable for the shoulders. Many pull-up bars include neutral grip handles.</p>

      <h3>Mixed Grip</h3>

      <p>One hand overhand, one hand underhand. Mixed grip turns the trunk slightly and your core resists twisting. Switch hand positions between sets. Keep the underhand elbow straight and stop if the front of the elbow hurts.</p>

      <h3>Weekly Grip Rotation</h3>

      <p class="table-cue">Scroll the table horizontally on smaller screens.</p><div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Scrollable comparison table" tabindex="0"><table class="data-table">
