      <p>The <a href="/deadhang-variations/">dead hang variations guide</a> covers more grip options.</p>

      <h2>Active Hang Training</h2>

      <p>An active hang engages the shoulder muscles during the hold. Your arms stay straight but your shoulder blades pull down and back. This works the lats, lower traps and serratus anterior.</p>

      <h3>How to Perform an Active Hang</h3>

      <ol>
        <li>Grip the bar and hang with straight arms (passive position)</li>
        <li>Pull your shoulder blades down toward your back pockets</li>
        <li>Keep your arms completely straight throughout</li>
        <li>Hold the engaged position for 20-30 seconds</li>
        <li>Release back to passive hang before dropping</li>
      </ol>

      <p>Active hangs can feel harder than passive hangs at first. Aim for 20 to 30 seconds of active hang between passive sets to build the shoulder stability that later training uses. The <a href="/passive-vs-active-hang/">passive vs active hang guide</a> covers technique for both.</p>

      <h3>Integrating Active and Passive Hangs</h3>

      <p>Alternate between passive and active sets within a single session. Start with 2 passive sets at your target time. Follow with 2 active sets at 20-30 seconds. The total session includes both grip endurance and shoulder stability work.</p>

      <h2>Programming for Intermediates</h2>

      <p>Three sessions a week is a common place to start. Alternate between grip types across sessions. The weekly grip rotation table above shows one arrangement with Monday, Wednesday and Friday sessions. Every second Friday the session can be swapped for a best-hold test.</p>

      <h3>Tracking Progress</h3>

      <p>The optional Friday test works as your progress check. Compare your test hold with the previous test using the same grip and bar. Add about 5 seconds to your target hold once you complete every set cleanly, roughly once a week. Test holds that stall for 2 or more weeks suggest a plateau.</p>

      <h2>Breaking Through Plateaus</h2>

      <p>A grip plateau means your hold times stop rising despite regular training. Five strategies are worth trying at the intermediate level.</p>

      <h3>1. Change Your Grip</h3>

      <p>Swap your primary grip for 2 weeks. If you always train overhand then switch to neutral or underhand as your main grip. The new stimulus forces fresh adaptation. Return to overhand after 2 weeks and retest.</p>

      <h3>2. Add Light Weight</h3>

      <p>Strap on 5-10 lbs with a dip belt for your regular sets. Reduce hold times to 15-20 seconds. Train with added weight for 2 weeks. Then remove it and retest your bodyweight hang.</p>

      <h3>3. Reduce Rest Periods</h3>

      <p>Cut rest from 60 seconds to 45 seconds between sets. The increased fatigue forces your grip to adapt to less recovery. Return to 60-second rest after 2 weeks.</p>

      <h3>4. Grease the Groove</h3>

      <p>Perform 5-8 sub-maximal hangs spread throughout the day. Each hang uses 50-60% of your best hold. Total daily volume rises without creating session fatigue. This works if you have a bar at home that you can use safely.</p>

      <h3>5. Deload and Retest</h3>

      <p>A lighter week about every fourth week is a common coaching convention, not a tested rule. Train at about 50% volume and intensity for one week with two sessions of 2 sets each. Then retest your hold.</p>

      <h2>Moving to Advanced</h2>

      <p>This site treats a 60-second overhand dead hang as the point where advanced dead hang training starts. This band is the site's own training range and not a published standard.</p>

      <h3>Signs You Are Ready</h3>

      <ul>
        <li>60+ second overhand dead hang on a fresh test</li>
        <li>Three grip types trained consistently for 4+ weeks</li>
        <li>Active hang held for 30+ seconds</li>
        <li>No joint pain during or after training</li>
      </ul>

      <p>When you meet these signs, <a href="/advanced-deadhang/">advanced dead hang training</a> covers weighted and one-arm work. The training programs page lists the 4, 8 and 12-week planning frameworks.</p>

