      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <div class="faq">
          <h3>How long does it take to go from a 30-second to a 90-second dead hang?</h3>
          <p>There is no published timeline. A target hold that rises about 5 seconds a week takes roughly 12 weeks to go from 30 to 90 seconds. Many people take longer because progress is rarely that steady. Progress depends on grip variation, volume and rest between sets.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq">
          <h3>Should I train different grip types or stick with overhand?</h3>
          <p>Rotating between overhand, underhand and neutral grips adds variety and changes wrist and shoulder position. Your finger flexors do the holding in all three. No study has compared rotating grips with overhand-only training for hang endurance.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq">
          <h3>What is the difference between a passive and active dead hang?</h3>
          <p>A passive hang lets your shoulders relax fully while you grip the bar. An active hang keeps your arms straight but engages the scapulae by pulling shoulder blades down and back. Active hangs build shoulder stability that supports advanced progression.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq">
          <h3>How do I break through a dead hang plateau at 45 seconds?</h3>
          <p>At a 45-second plateau, five strategies are worth trying at the intermediate level: switch your primary grip for 2 weeks, add 5-10 lbs with a dip belt for short holds, reduce rest periods to 45 seconds, spread sub-maximal hangs throughout the day, or take a lighter week at about 50% volume.</p>
        </div>

